{"source_id":"W2510835855","title":"Comparing note taking and test performance in methods and modes of note taking conditions","year":2016,"abstract":"Note taking is a common and effective practice among college students to support learning during lectures. Generally, there are two justifications for perceived benefits from note taking: the encoding function and the external storage function. The present study examined the effects of note taking method (longhand versus laptop) and the opportunity to review notes on test performance. Undergraduate participants (N = 116) viewed a video lecture and took notes either longhand or by laptop, then either reviewed their notes or did not review them before completing a test of factual and conceptual recall. Results showed a main effect of review, such that participants who reviewed their notes performed better than those who did not. However, there was no significant main effect of note taking method and no significant interaction between note taking method and review opportunity, indicating that the longhand advantage was not moderated by reviewing notes."}
